hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're going to be going  over the bioesculin test or the  bioesculin agar test  sometimes this auger is abbreviated bea  before we jump into it i want to make  sure you know two things first i have a  playlist on microbiology diagnostic  tests  that covers  all of the selective and differential  augers all of those tests like the  catalase test and the lecithinase test  and the coagulase test  and all of the different stains that you  need to be familiar with you're taking a  microbiology lab so if you're taking a  microbiology course definitely check out  that playlist it has like everything you  need  also  if you want to grab a picture of the  final board with all of the answers  filled in just grab it from the link in  the description below and that is your  free study notes  so now let's talk about bioesculent  auger it is selective and differential  what do these things mean selective  means that it allows some things to grow  while inhibiting others and then of the  things that it allows to grow the  differential aspect is that there are  ways to  to observe  basically metabolic differences to be  able to distinguish like one species  from another  so in the case of bile esculent agar it  is used to isolate and differentiate  enterococcus species  so specifically in pterococcus from  streptococcus species and other sorts of  gram-positive  cocci  so  how does it work well first it's got a  few different components we're going to  go over it has bile salts sometimes  you'll see these referred to as oxal or  ox bile  and those are just different words for  the same thing but basically different  bile salts these inhibit the growth of  most  gram-positive  bacteria  so if you have like a mixed culture  or you have  a culture of gram-positive bacteria  gram-positive spheres and you're not  sure what they are if you put them on  bio-escalant auger most of them are  going to be inhibited mainly except in  terracocci and maybe a few strep species  now this agar also contains peptic  digests of animal tissue  uh and or beef extract now these make it  what's known as a complex medium right  it's not chemically defined where every  single batch of bio-escalant agar is  exactly the same  um instead you know different lots of  animal tissue different lots of beef  extracts can have slightly different  you know macromolecule concentrations  and slightly different nutrient  concentrations and so it's what we call  a complex media  but the reason that the animal tissue or  beef extracts are included is because  they provide carbon  nitrogen  and other essential factors think things  like vitamins right  there's also auger  of course the auger is to make it a  solid media  and then it has two other kind of key  ingredients one is esculene  and the other  is called ferric  citrate  now it is the esculene and the ferric  citrate together that cause a color  change  that gives what we call a positive  reaction or a positive result in the  bioesculin test or the biolascular auger  test  this test is also usually done in slant  tubes  if you've not seen these before  basically it's where the molten media  you know the auger has been added  but it's still very very hot and so it's  a liquid molten consistency and it's  poured in tubes and then the tubes are  placed at an angle usually about a 45  degree angle while they cool and the  media solidifies and then the auger  that's inside even though it's a solid  auger  a solid auger medium it's at a slant so  that's most common for this type of test  now how does this test work  so remember we're looking at  enterococcus the pleural enterococci  formula formerly these were called group  d scrap so you might still see that in  some older resources  these produce  an enzyme called  esculenase  remember whenever a word ends in ace  that means it's an enzyme so esculenas  carries out this reaction  esculene  plus bile  with escalinase this enzyme escylnase  will hydrolyze  esculene  into a different compound escalatin  resculatin i hear it pronounced both  ways escalatin  plus glucose  okay and then this product esculeton  right here  reacts with ferric citrate and that was  the other important ingredient here so  the esculene gets hydrolyzed by that  esculanase enzyme and then the product  of that the escalatin reacts with the  ferric citrate this forms a phenol  precipitate that is a dark brown  or black  color  now  you might need to know this many  bacteria can hydrolyze esculene  but only a very few mainly the  enterococci can do so in the presence of  the bile salts so that's another way in  which the bile salts are  selective here  so here's the steps for how you would do  a bile escalant auger test you would  take a colony typically this is from a  blood agar plate i am preparing another  video that should come out in a few  weeks hopefully  on blood auger plates how they're made  and on the different hemolysis  patterns the different hemolytic  patterns that we see  in different types of bacteria  and so when we're looking at  these the enterococci remember they're  very closely related to strep to  streptococcus that's why they used to be  classified as streptococci  but these are typically going to be  growing on blood agar plates so you take  a colony  excuse me  take a colony from a blood agar plate  aseptically right that means without  introducing any kind of contamination  and then you streak it on the slant so  basically you're taking a sterile loop  picking up a colony and you want to see  if that colony is in terracoxide or  something else  and then you  streak it  you know  down the surface of the slant so it's a  streak down the surface of the auger  you're not puncturing the auger like you  do for some other tests  then um i'll go ahead and put a streak  here too to be for a different colony  and then this is important you leave the  caps loose  this allows oxygen to  be present in here to diffuse in so it  allows these bacteria ample oxygen for  aerobic respiration purposes  so that's why you have to leave the  capsules you incubate for 35 to 37  degrees celsius for 24 to 48 hours and  then you interpret based on the color  change  basically if there's no color change  none of this dark brown or black that's  considered a negative result an example  of a negative result would come from  streptococcus pyogenes  a positive result or a positive reaction  like you would get with enterococcus  spicalis  is where you see this dark brown or  black coloration forming and again  that's that precipitate  because the enterococci have the enzyme  that carries out this reaction and gets  this product to  react with ferric citrate and create  that color change  now  you see here i've kind of drawn this two  ways the the brown or black color it  could fill the entire auger especially  if you're using a smaller tube for your  slant or it might only  fill like the upper portion it just kind  of depends on how much activity there is  how many  enterococci cells there are  and the time that it takes for the  the precipitates to kind of diffuse  through the medium basically  remember this playlist right here  microbiology diagnostic tests you can  learn about  you know a dozen more different kinds of  augers that you may be studying in class  plus a lot of other diagnostic tests  catalase tests coagulase test lesson  finish test things like that so check  that out good luck studying grab your  free study notes at the link below that  will include a picture of the final  board  and i will see you next time thanks for  watching biology professor bye

